Yes. Acts are where a claim meets reality.

A useful hierarchy is:

words → intentions → actions → repeated actions → consequences

Each step carries more evidentiary weight than the one before it.

If someone says they value loyalty, one loyal act supports the claim. A pattern of loyal choices under inconvenience supports it much more strongly. If someone says they are disciplined, the validation is not the sentence—it is what they repeatedly do when discipline costs something.

But an act does not prove the entire inner state behind it. The same behavior can come from different motives. So the strongest test is:

stated value
→ behavior
→ consistency across contexts
→ cost willingly borne
→ outcomes
→ AAR / update

That is why action is such a good validator: it generates external evidence.

In Pantheon terms:

Enactment Test

idea → embodiment → consequence → delta → doctrine update

Or shorter:

Belief proposes. Action tests. Reality grades.